big cubic cage. As seen in Figure 1, the octahedral cage is constructed from two $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ clusters and four ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{TBPP}}$ ligands, with the cavity diameter of c.a. $1.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ . However, the cubic cage consists of eight $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ clusters as the vertices and six $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TBPP}$ ligands as the sides. In particular, the diameter of the cubic cage is approximately $2.5~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which is larger than that in the PCN-221 $(2.0\ \mathrm{nm})$ .11b Accordingly, the available volume is $15000\mathrm{~\AA~}^{3}$ . Additionally, the window of the cubic cage is $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}\times2.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which allows large organic molecules to freely get in and out.
## Gas adsorption experiments and stability tests
Calculation by $P L A T O N$ software14 reveals that in FJI-H6 the free volume is up to $78.6\%$ . For FJI-H6 the permanent porosity is confirmed by ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm measured at 77 K. The sample exchanged with acetone exhibits reversible type I isotherm and has a saturated uptake of $1346~{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}~{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at 1 atm (Figure 2). When pre-treated with $^\mathrm{~8~M~}$ HCl, the value of ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption slightly increases and gets to $1393~{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}~{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ , which indicates that FJI-H6 is stable to the acid. From the above data, the calculated BET surface area of the sample exchanged with acetone is up to $5007~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $(5033\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the sample pretreated with $8\ \mathrm{~M~HCl})$ , which is much larger than those of PCN-222(Fe) $(2200~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,4b NU-1000 $(2320~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,15 PCN223(Fe) $(1600\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,10c PCN-94 $(3377\mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,10b NU-1100 $(4020\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})^{10\mathrm{a}}$ and PCN-229 $(4619\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}\ \mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ ,13 but less than those of just reported NU-1103 $(5646~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ ) and NU-1104 $(5290~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ .10e In addition, FJI-H6 also has the high total pore volume of $2.16~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The experimental BET surface area and pore volume are consistent with theoretical ones calculated by Poreblazer16 (accessible surface area: $4695~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ; the pore volume: $2.06\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})^{14}$ , which demonstrates that the sample is fully activated. Additionally, FJI-H6 also shows good capacity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage. The $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake reaches 172 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $(1.54~\mathrm{wt\%})$ at 1 atm and $77~\mathrm{K}$ , and $108~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (0.94 $w t\%$ at $87~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm. Moreover, the adsorption heat of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ calculated by the Clausius–Clapeyron equation is $6.54~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ at zero coverage and decreases slowly along with the increasing $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ loading. These values are comparable to those of the famous MOFs materials, such as HKUST-1 $(6.6\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}.$ ),17 MOF-5 $(5.2\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}})$ ,17 and NOTT-122 $(6.0\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}},$ ).18 As for FJI-H7 the sample exchanged with acetone also exhibits reversible type I isotherm and has a saturated uptake of $1029~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1 atm. From the above data, the calculated BET surface area of FJIH7 is up to $3831~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is also among the highest of Hfbased MOFs reported.11
Since the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Hf_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ cluster are both highly connected with twelve carboxylate groups, FJI-H6 and FJI-H7 are expected to have high stability. To test their stability, we immersed the micro-crystal sample of FJI-H6 or FJI-H7 into the water with various $\mathsf{p H}$ values for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ (See Figure S2). The PXRD patterns of the resulting samples well match with the simulated one, which suggests that FJI-H6 and FJI-H7 retain their crystalline. Especially, FJI-H6 has high stability to the acid. As seen in Figure 2, both the curves and the adsorption values for the sample in $1\mathrm{~M~HCl}$ do not deviate much from those of the untreated sample.
Incorporating ${\bf{C u}}^{2+}$ ions into the open porphyrin rings via the SCSC transformations
Considering that in both FJI-H6 and FJI-H7 two nitrogen atoms of the porphyrin ring are un-deprotoned, we attempt to introduce the second kind of metal ions into the framework. Immersing the single-crystals of FJI-H6 or FJI-H7 into a solution of 0.5 M $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ in N,N-dimethyl formamide (DMF) at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ results in metallated FJI-H6(Cu) or FJI${\bf H}7({\bf C u})$ . As anticipated, single crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray structural analysis
